-- title:
--   Algebraic Hartogs lemma along a prime divisor
-- statement:
--   Let $B$ be a noetherian integrally closed domain, let $F$ be a field, and let $\iota\colon B \to F$ be a ring homomorphism that is injective as a function. Let $\varpi \in B$ be nonzero and such that the ideal generated by the singleton $\{\varpi\}$ is prime, and let $x \in F$. Assume two conditions: first, there exist $a, s \in B$ with $s \notin (\varpi)$ and $x\,\iota(s) = \iota(a)$; second, for every prime ideal $P$ of $B$ whose height equals $1$ and with $\varpi \notin P$, there exist $a, s \in B$ with $s \notin P$ and $x\,\iota(s) = \iota(a)$. The conclusion is that $x$ lies in the range of $\iota$, i.e. $x = \iota(b)$ for some $b \in B$. Thus regularity of $x$ at the single prime $(\varpi)$ together with regularity at all height-one primes avoiding $\varpi$ suffices; no hypothesis relates $F$ to $B$ beyond the injection, and in particular $F$ need not be the fraction field of $B$.
--
--   This is the algebraic Hartogs lemma $B = \bigcap_{\operatorname{ht} P = 1} B_P$ for a noetherian normal domain, reorganised so that the height-one primes are split into the single "vertical" prime $(\varpi)$ and the "horizontal" ones avoiding $\varpi$, and stated for an arbitrary injection of $B$ into a field rather than for its fraction field. It is used in the construction of the model at $p$ of a modular curve, to show that a section of a structure sheaf read off on a stalk comes from an integral element.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_IsIntegrallyClosed_mem_range_of_isPrime_span_of_forall_height_eq_one_of_notMem.lean

